1
00:00:01,530 --> 00:00:04,220
I bought a time machine for twelve
dollars.

2
00:00:04,388 --> 00:00:06,608
Now the Roman army wants me dead.

3
00:00:07,090 --> 00:00:10,730
And somehow, this is still not the worst
thing I've ever bought online.

4
00:00:11,302 --> 00:00:12,892
Okay. Let me explain.

5
00:00:16,360 --> 00:00:20,460
It started when I found a time machine
online for twelve dollars.

6
00:00:21,215 --> 00:00:22,835
The reviews were amazing.

7
00:00:23,070 --> 00:00:25,490
Five stars. Five stars. Five stars.

8
00:00:25,792 --> 00:00:29,393
And one guy saying it turned his dog into
a refrigerator.

9
00:00:30,182 --> 00:00:31,612
I assumed he was joking.

10
00:00:31,915 --> 00:00:33,675
So I pressed the big red button.

11
00:00:34,255 --> 00:00:35,745
One second, I was in my room.

12
00:00:36,710 --> 00:00:38,990
The next, I was in Ancient Rome.

13
00:00:39,930 --> 00:00:40,900
No Wi-Fi.

14
00:00:41,270 --> 00:00:42,080
No money.

15
00:00:42,450 --> 00:00:44,930
No idea what year it was.

16
00:00:45,702 --> 00:00:51,052
My phone had three percent battery, and my
time machine said it needed seven days to recharge.

17
00:00:51,740 --> 00:00:53,780
Seven days.

18
00:00:54,555 --> 00:00:57,525
I couldn't even survive seven minutes
without checking my phone.

19
00:00:58,230 --> 00:00:59,460
Then things got worse.

20
00:01:00,300 --> 00:01:03,020
A Roman soldier saw my phone flashlight.

21
00:01:03,255 --> 00:01:04,765
MAXIMUS: A WIZARD!

22
00:01:05,067 --> 00:01:10,447
And apparently, a tiny glowing rectangle was enough to
convince him I was some kind of powerful wizard.

23
00:01:11,288 --> 00:01:12,758
This was going to be a problem.

24
00:01:14,737 --> 00:01:18,967
By day two, I had discovered something
important about Ancient Rome.

25
00:01:19,337 --> 00:01:21,298
Everybody wanted money.

26
00:01:21,867 --> 00:01:23,818
And nobody accepted Apple Pay.

27
00:01:24,120 --> 00:01:26,300
I tried explaining digital payments.

28
00:01:26,535 --> 00:01:30,215
The woman selling bread handed me an
actual apple.

29
00:01:30,382 --> 00:01:31,702
Honestly, fair enough.

30
00:01:32,972 --> 00:01:34,142
Her name was Lucia.

31
00:01:34,445 --> 00:01:37,305
She offered me bread in exchange for
cleaning dishes.

32
00:01:37,607 --> 00:01:40,877
Finally. A job that matched my
qualifications.

33
00:01:41,382 --> 00:01:43,092
I had no qualifications.

34
00:01:47,637 --> 00:01:48,788
I even made a friend.

35
00:01:49,090 --> 00:01:52,300
A little kid who found everything I did
hilarious.

36
00:01:52,535 --> 00:01:53,605
Which was nice.

37
00:01:56,010 --> 00:01:59,240
Because back home, people usually just
found it concerning.

38
00:02:02,780 --> 00:02:05,250
On day three, I had a brilliant idea.

39
00:02:05,485 --> 00:02:06,765
I was from the future.

40
00:02:06,932 --> 00:02:08,892
I knew things these people didn't.

41
00:02:09,195 --> 00:02:13,535
So I decided to become rich by introducing
Ancient Rome to modern food.

42
00:02:13,702 --> 00:02:15,732
Specifically, pizza.

43
00:02:16,035 --> 00:02:17,745
There was one small problem.

44
00:02:17,980 --> 00:02:19,240
No tomatoes.

45
00:02:20,145 --> 00:02:23,685
I made something with dough, cheese, and
olives.

46
00:02:24,055 --> 00:02:25,235
Technically edible.

47
00:02:25,672 --> 00:02:27,352
Emotionally devastating.

48
00:02:28,427 --> 00:02:33,017
Lucia took one bite and stared at me like
I'd personally insulted her ancestors.

49
00:02:35,387 --> 00:02:37,857
So that was the end of my restaurant
empire.

50
00:02:40,027 --> 00:02:41,687
Then the soldier found me again.

51
00:02:41,787 --> 00:02:43,867
MAXIMUS: THAT'S HIM! The wizard!

52
00:02:44,102 --> 00:02:45,422
His name was Maximus.

53
00:02:45,792 --> 00:02:50,172
And he had apparently been telling
everyone about the magical glowing rectangle.

54
00:02:50,927 --> 00:02:51,587
Great.

55
00:02:52,427 --> 00:02:54,137
I'd been in Rome for three days.

56
00:02:54,372 --> 00:02:57,212
And I was already involved in a religious
misunderstanding.

57
00:03:00,622 --> 00:03:02,902
MAXIMUS: Show them your power!

58
00:03:03,205 --> 00:03:06,545
Maximus wanted me to demonstrate my
powers.

59
00:03:06,780 --> 00:03:07,880
In public.

60
00:03:08,048 --> 00:03:09,278
For an audience.

61
00:03:09,982 --> 00:03:13,892
Now, normally, I handle pressure very
well.

62
00:03:14,330 --> 00:03:15,600
That was a lie.

63
00:03:16,440 --> 00:03:17,680
My phone was dead.

64
00:03:17,915 --> 00:03:20,915
My time machine still had three days left.

65
00:03:21,218 --> 00:03:23,367
And everyone was staring at me.

66
00:03:25,802 --> 00:03:29,182
I grabbed a shiny tray and reflected
sunlight into his face.

67
00:03:29,283 --> 00:03:30,832
Pure science.

68
00:03:31,068 --> 00:03:32,628
Very advanced.

69
00:03:32,930 --> 00:03:36,380
Mostly because nobody here knew I was
improvising.

70
00:03:37,050 --> 00:03:39,240
Then a pigeon landed on his head.

71
00:03:41,675 --> 00:03:45,945
And for reasons I still don't understand,
everyone started cheering.

72
00:03:46,650 --> 00:03:48,870
Maximus declared me a wizard.

73
00:03:49,105 --> 00:03:52,965
Then he invited me to perform a miracle at
the city festival.

74
00:03:53,200 --> 00:03:54,650
In three days.

75
00:03:55,020 --> 00:03:59,080
I had accidentally become Rome's least
qualified magician.

76
00:04:00,890 --> 00:04:03,930
By day five, people were asking me to
predict the future.

77
00:04:04,165 --> 00:04:05,975
Which sounded easy.

78
00:04:06,210 --> 00:04:08,930
I was literally from the future.

79
00:04:09,300 --> 00:04:12,430
Unfortunately, I knew absolutely nothing
useful.

80
00:04:13,270 --> 00:04:15,720
One man wanted to know whether he'd become
rich.

81
00:04:15,955 --> 00:04:18,355
I told him to invest in property.

82
00:04:19,425 --> 00:04:21,205
Another wanted tomorrow's weather.

83
00:04:21,440 --> 00:04:22,540
I looked at the sky.

84
00:04:22,775 --> 00:04:23,745
Not a cloud.

85
00:04:24,115 --> 00:04:26,685
So I confidently predicted sunshine.

86
00:04:27,525 --> 00:04:30,845
For the first time in my life, I felt like
a genius.

87
00:04:31,215 --> 00:04:34,045
That feeling lasted about eleven seconds.

88
00:04:37,180 --> 00:04:38,460
Day six.

89
00:04:38,763 --> 00:04:39,603
It rained.

90
00:04:39,973 --> 00:04:40,903
A lot.

91
00:04:41,743 --> 00:04:46,903
Apparently, people don't enjoy paying for
a weather forecast that's completely wrong.

92
00:04:47,205 --> 00:04:49,755
Especially when the forecast comes from a
wizard.

93
00:04:50,460 --> 00:04:52,500
Maximus was furious.

94
00:04:52,803 --> 00:04:57,243
He said that tomorrow, at the festival, I
would have to prove I could control the weather.

95
00:04:57,478 --> 00:04:59,308
Or I'd be thrown in prison.

96
00:04:59,678 --> 00:05:02,028
This was a slight career setback.

97
00:05:02,968 --> 00:05:04,848
Luckily, I had a plan.

98
00:05:05,083 --> 00:05:06,463
A terrible plan.

99
00:05:06,698 --> 00:05:08,618
But technically a plan.

100
00:05:08,988 --> 00:05:12,338
I'd hide a bucket above the stage, pull a
rope, and make it rain.

101
00:05:12,573 --> 00:05:13,463
Fake rain.

102
00:05:13,698 --> 00:05:14,898
But still rain.

103
00:05:15,803 --> 00:05:17,233
Lucia looked at the machine.

104
00:05:17,400 --> 00:05:18,230
Then at me.

105
00:05:18,398 --> 00:05:19,638
Then back at the machine.

106
00:05:20,008 --> 00:05:21,198
She didn't say anything.

107
00:05:21,568 --> 00:05:23,958
Which, somehow, was worse than yelling.

108
00:05:28,703 --> 00:05:33,733
After six hours of engineering, I had
successfully invented getting hit by a bucket.

109
00:05:36,978 --> 00:05:38,188
Day seven.

110
00:05:38,423 --> 00:05:39,423
The festival.

111
00:05:39,793 --> 00:05:44,163
Hundreds of Romans had gathered to witness
my incredible powers.

112
00:05:44,868 --> 00:05:46,318
I had one bucket.

113
00:05:46,620 --> 00:05:48,100
One rope.

114
00:05:48,403 --> 00:05:50,933
And approximately zero confidence.

115
00:05:51,638 --> 00:05:52,608
The good news?

116
00:05:52,775 --> 00:05:55,175
My time machine had almost finished
recharging.

117
00:05:55,478 --> 00:05:56,878
The bad news?

118
00:05:57,045 --> 00:05:59,825
I needed to survive another ten minutes.

119
00:06:00,195 --> 00:06:04,445
And judging by Maximus's expression, that
was ambitious.

120
00:06:04,680 --> 00:06:07,610
MAXIMUS: Now, wizard. Make it RAIN!

121
00:06:08,180 --> 00:06:09,610
I raised my hands.

122
00:06:09,845 --> 00:06:11,685
I stared into the sky.

123
00:06:11,920 --> 00:06:15,880
I made the mysterious face of a man who
definitely knew what he was doing.

124
00:06:16,385 --> 00:06:17,245
And then...

125
00:06:17,750 --> 00:06:18,640
Nothing.

126
00:06:19,145 --> 00:06:20,175
Not a drop.

127
00:06:23,380 --> 00:06:24,310
Wonderful.

128
00:06:24,680 --> 00:06:28,410
I had officially disappointed an entire
civilization.

129
00:06:29,755 --> 00:06:31,355
Maximus grabbed me.

130
00:06:31,523 --> 00:06:33,193
I grabbed the rope.

131
00:06:33,360 --> 00:06:35,180
The rope grabbed the bucket.

132
00:06:35,348 --> 00:06:37,458
And gravity handled the rest.

133
00:06:38,293 --> 00:06:42,083
For one beautiful second, I made it rain.

134
00:06:42,385 --> 00:06:44,125
On exactly one person.

135
00:06:44,428 --> 00:06:46,618
The angriest man in Ancient Rome.

136
00:06:49,888 --> 00:06:52,388
He did not appreciate the miracle.

137
00:06:53,190 --> 00:06:54,970
So I ran.

138
00:06:59,105 --> 00:07:01,155
I was being chased by Roman soldiers.

139
00:07:02,790 --> 00:07:04,560
My shoes were falling apart.

140
00:07:04,995 --> 00:07:08,435
And the world's cheapest time machine was
finally waking up.

141
00:07:08,670 --> 00:07:10,040
There was just one problem.

142
00:07:10,410 --> 00:07:11,960
Maximus had taken it.

143
00:07:12,900 --> 00:07:15,810
Then something incredible happened.

144
00:07:16,112 --> 00:07:18,093
Remember that kid I shared my bread with?

145
00:07:18,463 --> 00:07:24,003
Apparently, one piece of bread was enough to
buy the best emergency rescue service in Rome.

146
00:07:24,238 --> 00:07:24,938
BOY: Catch!

147
00:07:25,038 --> 00:07:27,038
He grabbed my watch and threw it back to
me.

148
00:07:27,273 --> 00:07:31,782
For the first time all week, a decision
I'd made had actually worked.

149
00:07:32,488 --> 00:07:33,458
Three seconds.

150
00:07:33,895 --> 00:07:34,535
Two.

151
00:07:34,972 --> 00:07:35,602
One.

152
00:07:36,190 --> 00:07:37,180
I pressed the button.

153
00:07:37,280 --> 00:07:38,350
The portal opened.

154
00:07:38,450 --> 00:07:39,293
I jumped.

155
00:07:39,343 --> 00:07:40,833
And Maximus grabbed my hoodie.

156
00:07:45,582 --> 00:07:47,952
I survived seven days in Ancient Rome.

157
00:07:48,255 --> 00:07:49,915
I escaped an entire army.

158
00:07:50,420 --> 00:07:51,650
And somehow...

159
00:07:52,425 --> 00:07:54,655
I brought the army with me.

160
00:07:55,295 --> 00:07:58,265
My twelve-dollar time machine had turned
into a group booking.

161
00:08:03,070 --> 00:08:04,520
I should've read the reviews.
